The best feasible recipe is the airbench96-style net at 9 epochs: 0.7542–0.7552 accuracy in about 8.26 s. That margin over 0.753 is very thin. The systems changes failed (E005 and E007 refuted H2.2.1 and H2.1), and dropping to 8.5 epochs missed the bar (E006). The lever that remains is sample efficiency per epoch. Lookahead alone was worth about 1.1 pp (E008), which shows that optimizer and averaging dynamics matter a great deal in this regime. The question: in very short CIFAR training (about 5–10 epochs, small ResNet/airbench-style CNNs), does Muon or another orthogonalized optimizer reach a given accuracy in fewer epochs or less wall-clock time than tuned Nesterov SGD with lookahead? Specifically:
- which hyperparameters work (lr, momentum, Newton-Schulz steps, batch size, which parameters get Muon);
- whether it combines with lookahead or EMA weight averaging;
- how large the gain is on CIFAR-100 compared with CIFAR-10.
This directly tests H1.2 / H1.2.1 (confidence 0.40–0.50, untested). The answer decides whether the next experiments port airbench94_muon's optimizer or stay with SGD and tune averaging or the schedule.
